{:tier :glm-5.2, :verse 5, :comments ["<b>And Abram took Sarai his wife and Lot his brother's son.</b> And above (Genesis 11:31) it is written, ״וַיִּקַּח תֶּרַח אֶת אַבְרָם בְּנוֹ וְאֶת לוֹט בֶּן הָרָן בֶּן בְּנוֹ וְאֵת שָׂרַי כַּלָּתוֹ״. Because before Abram was promised that he would have children, he would distance himself from Sarah on account of her being barren, for all those acts of relations were not for the perpetuation of the species. Therefore it is stated, ״וְתְהִי שָׂרַי עֲקָרָה אֵין לָהּ וָלָד״, and immediately adjacent to it, ״וַיִּקַּח תֶּרַח אֶת אַבְרָם״ etc. — because she was barren, therefore he placed Lot between them. But after it was stated, ״וְאֶעֶשְׂךָ לְגוֹי גָּדוֹל״, and he was promised children, it is written, ״וַיִּקַּח אַבְרָם אֶת שָׂרַי אִשְׁתּוֹ וְאֶת לוֹט״ — for the expression ״וַיִּקַּח״ indicates as though he took her as a second wife, in place of having been separated from her, in the manner stated (Exodus 2:1), ״וַיֵּלֶךְ אִישׁ מִבֵּית לֵוִי וַיִּקַּח אֶת בַּת לֵוִי״. And as for what is stated, ״וַיֵּלֶךְ אַבְרָם כַּאֲשֶׁר דִּבֶּר אֵלָיו ה׳ וַיֵּלֶךְ אִתּוֹ לוֹ״ — this means that Lot went after him of his own accord, but Abram did not bring him near; on the contrary, he kept him at a distance, and took Sarai his wife etc. Another matter: Terach would bring near those most closely related to him; therefore he first took Abram his son, and afterward took Lot his grandson's son, for the children of one's children are as one's children, and afterward took his daughter-in-law Sarai. But Abram first took Sarai his wife, for his wife is as his own body, and afterward took Lot, who is not as his own body."], :book :genesis, :voice :kli-yakar, :lang :en, :chapter 12, :notes {}, :source-witnesses [{"versionTitle" "Sefaria community Hebrew (PD)", "language" "he"}], :timestamp "2026-07-30T13:28:04.249559052Z", :translated-from :he, :model "glm-5.2"}